<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as J. K. Paasikivi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juho Kusti Paasikivi (1870–1956) was a Finnish statesman who served as prime minister in 1944–1946 and as president from 1946 to 1956. A conservative banker and veteran negotiator with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, who led the Finnish delegation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> in 1920 and in the talks before the Winter War, he built the post-war policy of neutrality and trust with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the so-called Paasikivi line, sealed by the treaty of 1948 and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen">Urho Kekkonen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi.jpg" alt="Juho Kusti Paasikivi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F.E Fremling · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J.K._Paasikivi_1945_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
